  L('m2c9-3', 'm2c9', 'Bag of words and n-grams',
`**Bag of words (BoW)** represents a document as a vector of **word counts** (or presence 0/1) over a fixed vocabulary. Word order is ignored.

• Simple and effective for classification.
• Drawbacks: very high-dimensional and **sparse**; loses **word order and context** ("bank raised rates" = "rates raised bank"); no notion of synonyms.

**n-grams** are sequences of n consecutive tokens: unigrams (1), **bigrams** (2: "interest rate"), trigrams (3).
• Capture some local context and phrases ("not good", "credit default swap").
• Vocabulary grows quickly with n → even more sparsity.

Example: "rates rise fast" → unigrams {rates, rise, fast}; bigrams {rates rise, rise fast}. A sentence of k words has k − n + 1 n-grams.`,
['BoW: counts, order ignored, sparse', 'n-grams capture local word order', 'k words → k − n + 1 n-grams', 'Larger n → bigger, sparser vocabulary']);
