  L('m2c9-6', 'm2c9', 'Naive Bayes classifier for documents',
`**Naive Bayes** applies **Bayes' theorem** with the "naive" assumption that words are **conditionally independent** given the class:

P(class | words) ∝ P(class) × Π P(wordᵢ | class)

• **Prior** P(class): share of training documents in the class.
• **Likelihoods** P(word | class): estimated from word frequencies in each class; use **Laplace (add-one) smoothing** so unseen words do not give zero probability.
• Choose the class with the highest posterior (in practice sum log-probabilities to avoid underflow).

Strengths: fast, needs little data, works well for spam filtering, sentiment and topic classification, good baseline. Weaknesses: the independence assumption is unrealistic; probabilities are often poorly calibrated (too extreme).

Example: P(neg) = 0.4, P("loss"|neg) = 0.3; P(pos) = 0.6, P("loss"|pos) = 0.05. Scores: neg 0.12, pos 0.03 → classify negative (posterior 0.12/0.15 = 80%).`,
