  L('m2c10-2', 'm2c10', 'Embeddings as word vectors: advantages and limitations',
`An **embedding** maps each word (or token) to a **dense vector** of a few hundred real numbers, learned so that words used in similar contexts get similar vectors ("you shall know a word by the company it keeps").

Compared with one-hot / BoW:
• **Dense and low-dimensional** (e.g. 300 dimensions instead of a 50,000-word vocabulary).
• Capture **semantic similarity** and relationships (bank ≈ lender).
• Reusable: pretrained embeddings transfer to new tasks.

Limitations:
• **Static** embeddings (Word2Vec, GloVe) give **one vector per word**, so they cannot separate meanings (river *bank* vs financial *bank*) → **contextual embeddings** from transformers (BERT) solve this.
