  L('m2c10-10', 'm2c10', 'Challenges and methods in evaluating GenAI and LLMs',
`**Why it is hard**: open-ended outputs have many acceptable answers; quality is multi-dimensional (accuracy, relevance, coherence, safety, tone); outputs are non-deterministic; **hallucinations** look fluent; benchmarks can leak into training data (**contamination**); behaviour changes across versions and prompts.

**Methods**
• **Human evaluation**: expert ratings, pairwise comparisons — gold standard, but slow, costly, subjective.
• **Automated reference-based metrics**: **BLEU** (n-gram precision, translation), **ROUGE** (n-gram recall, summaries), BERTScore (embedding similarity).
• **Perplexity**: how well the model predicts held-out text (lower = better); intrinsic, not task quality.
• **Benchmarks**: standard test sets (e.g. MMLU), domain-specific test suites.
• **LLM-as-a-judge**: another model grades outputs — scalable, but with its own biases.
• **Task-specific checks**: factual consistency with source, citation accuracy, toxicity/bias tests, red-teaming, robustness to prompt variations, monitoring in production with user feedback.`,
